    "project_template_level_name": "aif-y2-natural-language-processing-lesson13-choice1-2026 + template",
    "long_instructions": "Remember `model` is a dictionary that records which tokens follow which. Each key is a token. Its value is a list of every token that came right after it in the corpus, including repeats. For example:\r\n\r\n```\r\n{\r\n  \"The\": [\"pen\", \"customer\", \"proof\", …],\r\n  \"pen\": [\"is\"],\r\n  \"is\": [\"mightier\", \"always\", \"in\", \"in\", …],\r\n  …\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe `learn` function fills in `model`. It’s called once for each sentence in the corpus. Its argument, `tokens`, is the list of tokens in a sentence.\r\n\r\n**☑️ Do This:**\r\n\r\n- Complete the `learn` function. For each pair of neighboring tokens in `tokens`, add the second token to the first token’s list in `model`. \r\n- If the first token isn’t a key in `model` yet, add it as a key with a new list containing the second token.\r\n- If the first token is already a key, append the second token to its list.\r\n\r\n**💻 Coding Tip**\r\n\r\nA `for` loop can give you each pair of neighboring tokens:\r\n\r\n```\r\nfor i in range(len(tokens) - 1):\r\n  token = tokens[i]\r\n  next_token = tokens[i + 1]\r\n```\r\n\r\n`in` checks whether a dictionary already has a key:\r\n\r\n```\r\nif token not in model:\r\n  ...\r\nelse:\r\n  ...\r\n```",
    "bubble_choice_description": "A collection of short sayings that express some truth.",
